    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d91">

                <pb facs="0280" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg156"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 294/158: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Herrick</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-04-03T13:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 3, 1923—1
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>133. For Wadsworth. W–15.</p>
                <p>This Government does not concur in the proposal suggested. It is felt that in any
                    contingency the agreement should be effective at least in respect of that
                    portion of future payments attributable to the Governments who are participating
                    in the present negotiations and that it should not be in any way dependent upon
                    the attitude of the other powers mentioned. I am unable to see any reason why
                        <pb facs="0281" n="157" xml:id="pg157"/> the substantial settlement of our
                    claim should be dependent on the attitude of these other powers. At all events
                    the Allies who have been paid at our expense have the responsibility to arrange
                    this matter suitably.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
